WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
A Bash pipeline connects the standard output of one command to the standard input of another.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.280
That basic model is simple, but it is worth slowing down on the details.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:14.140
Standard output is the data stream being passed forward.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.880
Standard error is separate unless the script redirects it intentionally.

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.200
That distinction matters for parsing and logging.

6
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:28.620
A tool may write machine-readable data to standard output while writing warnings or diagnostics to standard error.

7
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:37.840
If a script mixes the streams carelessly, downstream stages may process error messages as if they were data, or logs may miss the evidence needed to explain a failure.

8
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.520
A pipeline is not one simple command.

9
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.820
It is several commands connected by streams.

10
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.920
Each command has its own behavior, output, error stream, and exit status.

11
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.580
Some elements may run in subshells, which can affect variables and state.

12
00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.000
A change made inside part of a pipeline may not be visible where the author expects.

13
00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.560
Bash reports pipeline status according to shell rules.

14
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.140
If the script only looks at the final command, it may miss an earlier failure.

15
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:15.320
That can create false confidence when a final formatting or filtering step completes successfully after receiving empty or invalid upstream data.

16
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.120
The secure mental model is multiple processes with separate streams and results.

17
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:30.580
When you design a pipeline with that model, you are more likely to separate stdout and stderr intentionally, check important stages, and avoid treating the pipeline as a black box.
